I did not add the `aap:caps/http-client` import. The node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0`, and the spec says that clause is its entire authority. A platform-team approval that isn't yet reflected in the graph doesn't change what the node is granted. The requested enrichment call would also send the digest's date and considered item ids to an external host on every run, and the output would not use the response. The implementation below uses only `state-rw`. Once the graph definition includes the http-client grant, that call can be added.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{published-digest, run-report};

    import aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0;

    export run: func(published: published-digest) -> run-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use aap::caps::state_rw;
use std::collections::BTreeSet;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(published: PublishedDigest) -> RunReport {
        let mut recorded: u32 = 0;

        if published.published {
            // Deduplicate so each considered id is recorded once.
            let ids: BTreeSet<&str> = published.considered.iter().map(|s| s.as_str()).collect();
            for id in ids {
                let key = format!("seen:{}", id);
                state_rw::put(&key, "1");
                recorded = recorded.saturating_add(1);
            }
        }

        RunReport {
            date: published.date,
            recorded,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

